# Skills that this repository ships but did NOT author.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE MEANS
#
# Two things, and the second is the one that was missing:
#
# 1. install_skills.sh names anything installed-but-not-produced-here under
#    "Installed but not produced here". A name listed here says that is
#    expected, and stops it being reported every run.
#
# 2. It is the repository's statement of provenance. An empty file is a claim
#    that everything under skills/ originates here -- not a default, and not
#    the absence of a claim. This repository is public and MIT-licensed, so
#    that claim is made to everyone who installs it.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RESOLVED (DG-263) -- four vendored skills were REMOVED, not licensed
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# These four were tracked here and shipping, and none of them was ours:
#
#   skills/workflow/debug-mantra/SKILL.md
#   skills/workflow/post-mortem/SKILL.md
#   skills/workflow/scrutinize/SKILL.md
#   skills/leadership/management-talk/SKILL.md
#
# All four were byte-identical to a third-party skill pack, `9arm-skills`,
# whose .claude-plugin/plugin.json lists exactly these four as its entire
# contents:
#
#   {"name": "9arm-skills", "skills": ["./skills/engineering/debug-mantra",
#    "./skills/engineering/post-mortem", "./skills/engineering/scrutinize",
#    "./skills/productivity/management-talk"]}
#
# So this was not four coincidental name collisions. It was one plugin,
# vendored. The pack carries NO LICENCE FILE, it is not a git checkout, and
# nothing in the four files carries an attribution line -- so there was no
# upstream to read terms from and no way to settle this from what was on disk.
#
# Option (b) of the three written here previously was taken: they are removed.
# Four skills is a smaller loss than shipping unlicensed work under MIT from a
# public repository. They are recorded in RETIRED.md and kept out of git.
#
# Nothing regressed operationally. The four installed under ~/.claude/skills/
# were symlinks pointing INTO that pack rather than at this repository, so what
# a session actually loaded had never been this repo's copy.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE PART THIS REPOSITORY CANNOT UNDO -- read this before reusing history
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The four entered at a ROOT commit of this repository, `6777149` (2026-06-22),
# under the flat paths `skills/<name>/SKILL.md`, and were moved into their
# category directories later. Removing them cleans the working tree and every
# release from that point on; it does NOT remove them from git history, and
# rewriting 486 of 487 commits to do so was judged the larger harm.
#
# So, stated plainly, because nobody should have to infer it from a diff:
#
#   Copies of those four files remain reachable in this repository's git
#   history, at commits at or before the one that removed them. They are the
#   work of the `9arm-skills` author, they are NOT this project's to license,
#   and the MIT LICENSE at the root of this repository DOES NOT COVER THEM.
#   Do not copy them out of history and treat them as MIT.
#
# If you are the author of that pack and you are reading this: get in touch via
# the address in SECURITY.md and we will do whatever you would prefer here --
# attribution, or purging them from history outright.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THIS FILE ONCE SAID THE OPPOSITE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# It said these four "were listed here as third-party with no source. Their
# source was the sibling repo all along... They are first-party now."
#
# Half of that was right and the conclusion was backwards. The sibling repo did
# have copies -- because the sibling repo had vendored them too. Finding a
# second copy of somebody else's work does not make it yours. The ORIGINAL
# entry, declaring them third-party, was correct; the merge resolved it in the
# wrong direction and this file claimed authorship of them for months.
#
# This was the same failure the merge was meant to end -- a surface asserting
# something nobody had checked -- with the sign flipped.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Add a name below only when a skill is genuinely authored elsewhere AND its
# terms are known. One name per line.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
